Z||000008||mrfixit58||07-23-2000||06:01 PM||||I have the same problem. Please let us know if the alignment cures the problem. If so, I need to replace the struts anyway but, it would still be nice to know.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||2
Z||000009||batboy||07-23-2000||07:29 PM||||When I bought my first Fiero over 4 years ago, it was also wearing out the back tires too fast. I had it aligned and that solved the problem. In my case, it appears the previous owner put new struts on, but never had it aligned.||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||07-23-2000||09:44 PM||||I'd check the control arm bushings, too.  The alignment shop should catch it if they are bad, but you might be stuck with a big bill if they do the work.<BR>My 86 GT would wear out rear tires in 20,000 miles, even with an alignment every year.  The insides would wear down to the cord.  Eagle GT, Eagle GT+4, and a no-name brand, it didn't matter.  Since I put poly bushings and a rear anti-sway bar on, I've gone 46,000, and they're just getting to the wear bars, nice and even all the way across.  Same alignment specs as always.  BFG Comp T/A HR4 now.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||nivlek68||07-23-2000||11:00 PM||||well post a.s.p thanks guys    nivlek<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Bruce||07-24-2000||01:30 AM||||I had the same problem on my 86 GT. An alignment fixed it. This sounds like a simple statement, but you have to remember that these cars need to have ALL the wheels aligned when you have it done. Otherwise, the problem won't be corrected, probably a little worse.||||reg||1
